Antibiotic-resistant hospital infections are a threat to vulnerable hospitalised patients. Hospital-acquired infections, often severe and long-lasting, add to this, as patients are under stress and their immune systems may be suppressed. Frequent and inappropriate use of antibiotics in clinical settings drives antibiotic resistance, leading to the emergence of multi-drug resistant strains, which can spread in hospitals and communities and complicate treatment, increasing morbidity and mortality. Addressing antibiotic resistance requires strengthening infection control, more targeted antibiotic usage and more investment in research to develop new antimicrobial therapies and vaccines. Enhanced infection control works when it is funded, and hand hygiene compliance in most hospitals sits around 40 percent. New antimicrobial therapies and vaccines are the long-term answer, and the research pipeline has been thin since the 1980s.
