Essential tremor (ET) is the most commonly encountered movement disorder in clinical practice. In the last decade, there has been significant progress in the understanding of the epidemiology and etiopathogenesis of ET. The management of ET remains a challenge. Oral therapeutic options are limited. The two first-line medications are primidone, which acts on the gamma amino butyric acid-ergic (GABA-ergic) system and propranolol, a beta blocker. Primidone generally leads to complete suppression of tremor in approximately half of ET patients. The ADRs of propranolol, however, are bothersome, and thus, this medication is often poorly tolerated. In a third of ET patients, tremors continue to persist, despite pharmacotherapy. Most of ET patients reduce or discontinue the medication, probably due to suboptimal medication efficacy and/ or the bothersome ADRs.
